<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Goliath</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Goliath is the Philistine giant of Gath in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, killed by the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">sling</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-elah">Valley of Elah</a> in the story told in 1 Samuel 17. The episode became a proverbial image of a weak opponent defeating a strong one. An inscription of the 10th or 9th century BCE found at Tell es-Safi, the site of Gath, contains two names etymologically similar to Goliath.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/goliath.jpg" alt="Goliath" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osmar Schindler (1869-1927) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osmar_Schindler_David_und_Goliath.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Goliath is attested in the Biblical narratives of 1 and 2 Samuel. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls</a> text of Samuel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a> give his height as four cubits and a span, about 2.06 metres, while the Masoretic Text has six cubits and a span, about 2.97 metres. Many scholars think the smaller number grew in transmission. The name Goliath is not Semitic and has been linked with that of the Lydian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alyattes">Alyattes</a>. Tell es-Safi, the biblical Gath, was one of the largest Philistine cities until its destruction in the 9th century BCE. A potsherd found there, dated between the 10th and mid-9th centuries BCE, bears the names ʾLWT and WLT, which are etymologically related to Goliath.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1 Samuel 17 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> face the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-elah">Valley of Elah</a>. For forty days Goliath comes out between the lines each morning and evening and challenges the Israelites to send a champion to decide the matter in single combat. Saul is afraid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, a young shepherd, accepts, declines Saul's armour and takes his staff, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">sling</a> and five stones from a brook.</p><p>Goliath curses him by his gods, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> answers that the battle is the Lord's. David's stone strikes Goliath in the forehead and David cuts off his head with Goliath's own sword. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a> flee, pursued as far as Gath and the gates of Ekron. Many scholars see the purpose of the story as showing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a>, a head taller than anyone in Israel yet unwilling to take the challenge, was unfit to be king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Another verse, 2 Samuel 21:19, says that Elhanan, son of Jaare-oregim, the Bethlehemite, killed Goliath the Gittite. Most scholars regard this as the older account, which the authors of the Deuteronomistic history reworked to credit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>.</p><p>The armour described in 1 Samuel 17 resembles Greek armour of the 6th century BCE, and a similar story appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> recalls conquering the giant Ereuthalion. Other scholars hold that the description fits a Philistine warrior of the 10th century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Babylonian Talmud</a> makes Goliath a son of Orpah, the sister-in-law of Ruth. The phrase <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and Goliath has come to describe a smaller, weaker opponent facing a much stronger one.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Goliath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the four corners the doubled spandrels show the Brazen Serpent, the Punishment of Haman, Judith and Holofernes, and David and <b>Goliath</b>, stories of the salvation of Israel.</blockquote><a href="/work/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bible tells of its king Achish sheltering David and of the giant <b>Goliath</b>; an inscription found at the site contains two names resembling Goliath.</blockquote><a href="/state/gath">Gath</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gath, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-elah">Valley of Elah</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.7,34.85" aria-label="Gath on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Goliath"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5352 4847h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5352 4847h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.7,34.85">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
